The Network Card (NIC) inspects the Ethernet Frame:

Integrity Check (CRC/FCS): The NIC calculates the Cyclic Redundancy Check (CRC) over the frame and compares it to the value in the Frame Trailer.

If corrupted: The frame is silently dropped immediately (hardware level).

If valid: Processing continues.

MAC Address Verification: The NIC checks the Destination MAC address:

If it matches the NIC's own hardware MAC address (or a broadcast/multicast address), the NIC accepts it.

If it belongs to a different device, it drops the frame to avoid wasting CPU cycles.

De-encapsulation: The NIC strips off the Layer 2 Ethernet Header and Trailer.

Multiplexing / Protocol Identification: It reads the EtherType field (e.g., 0x0800 for IPv4) to determine which Layer 3 protocol handler in the OS kernel should receive the remaining Packet.
